1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,700
This lesson builds an interview-ready mental model for Topological Sort, with emphasis on worked interview problem. The goal

2
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:27,450
is not to memorize a code shape. The goal is to recognize when the pattern is justified, name

3
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:41,200
the invariant before implementation, and explain why every pointer, state update, or recursive step preserves correctness. The core

4
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:54,950
mental model is simple: Order a directed acyclic graph so every prerequisite appears before what depends on it.

5
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:08,700
During an interview, say the model out loud before touching the editor. That gives the interviewer a testable

6
00:01:08,750 --> 00:01:22,450
plan and gives you a reference point when edge

7
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:36,200
cases appear. Strong cues include prerequisites, build order, dependency scheduling, and detect directed cycle. These phrases are evidence,

8
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:49,950
not proof. Translate the prompt into three questions: what part of the input is active, what summary changes

9
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:03,700
incrementally, and what decision lets you discard work permanently? If those answers line up with this pattern, proceed.

10
00:02:03,750 --> 00:02:17,450
If the prompt requires arbitrary reordering, nonlocal updates, or a guarantee the pattern cannot provide, choose another tool.

11
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:31,200
A disciplined candidate explains both why a pattern fits and what would make it fail. Write the invariant

12
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:44,950
in one sentence: Every queued node currently has zero

13
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:58,700
unresolved incoming dependencies. Treat that sentence as a contract. Before the loop or recursion begins, show why it

14
00:02:58,750 --> 00:03:12,450
is true. For every update, explain why it remains true. At termination, connect it directly to the returned

15
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:26,200
answer. This structure turns code narration into a short correctness proof. It also exposes common bugs early: if

16
00:03:26,250 --> 00:03:39,950
a variable cannot be connected to the invariant, it may be unnecessary or updated at the wrong time.

17
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:53,700
Use this sequence: build adjacency and indegree, seed all zero-indegree nodes, decrement each outgoing edge once, and compare

18
00:03:53,750 --> 00:04:07,450
output count with node count. Start with the clearest

19
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:21,200
correct implementation, then discuss optimization. Name boundary conventions explicitly. If duplicates, empty inputs, or invalid parameters change behavior,

20
00:04:21,250 --> 00:04:34,950
decide that behavior before coding. Keep the implementation vocabulary aligned with your explanation: names such as left, right,

21
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:48,700
distance, parent, count, or best should describe their role, not their type. This makes the dry run and

22
00:04:48,750 --> 00:05:02,450
test discussion much faster. Use this representative example: Course Schedule succeeds when Kahn's algorithm emits all courses; a

23
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:16,200
shorter output proves a cycle. Before calculating, predict what the state should mean after the first meaningful step.

24
00:05:16,250 --> 00:05:29,950
Then trace at least one normal transition, one boundary

25
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:43,700
transition, and one transition that changes the best answer. Do not narrate every assignment. Narrate the decisions that

26
00:05:43,750 --> 00:05:57,450
rule out alternatives. In a live interview, a small table with input position, active state, and current answer

27
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:11,200
is usually enough to make the reasoning visible. The target analysis is: O(V + E) time and space.

28
00:06:11,250 --> 00:06:24,950
Explain the bound by counting operations, not by pointing at the visual nesting of loops. A nested loop

29
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:38,700
can still be linear when a pointer moves only forward across the entire execution. Conversely, a short implementation

30
00:06:38,750 --> 00:06:52,450
can hide expensive copies, slicing, sorting, or repeated hashing.

31
00:06:52,500 --> 00:07:06,200
State time, auxiliary space, output space when relevant, and the assumptions behind the bound. The most common mistakes

32
00:07:06,250 --> 00:07:19,950
are reversing edge direction, missing isolated nodes, duplicate-edge indegree errors, and using undirected cycle logic. Test each one

33
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:33,700
deliberately. Use an empty input, a single item, all-equal values, no valid answer, an answer at the first

34
00:07:33,750 --> 00:07:47,450
or last position, and the smallest input that forces the main state transition. For numeric problems, add zero,

35
00:07:47,500 --> 00:08:01,200
negative values, and overflow-sized totals when the prompt permits them. A good final check is to restate the

36
00:08:01,250 --> 00:08:14,950
invariant and verify every mutation preserves it. A concise

37
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:28,700
talk track is: I see Topological Sort because the prompt contains the relevant structural cues. My state will

38
00:08:28,750 --> 00:08:42,450
represent the active portion of the input, and my invariant is the contract I just stated. I will

39
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:56,200
update one boundary or decision at a time, restore validity when necessary, and record the answer only when

40
00:08:56,250 --> 00:09:09,950
the invariant guarantees it is meaningful. The resulting complexity follows from the bounded number of state transitions. I

41
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:23,700
will dry-run a boundary case before I call the solution complete. Practice in three passes. First, classify six

42
00:09:23,750 --> 00:09:37,450
prompts without coding and write only the state and

43
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:51,200
invariant. Second, implement two canonical problems from a blank editor while speaking the proof. Third, take one near-miss

44
00:09:51,250 --> 00:10:04,950
problem where this pattern looks tempting but is wrong, and explain the disqualifying condition. Revisit the set after

45
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:18,700
forty-eight hours. A pattern counts as learned only when you can identify it, reject it when inappropriate, implement

46
00:10:18,750 --> 00:10:32,450
it, test it, and explain its complexity without notes. Finish by writing one new edge case and predicting

47
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:46,200
the result before running code; that prediction is evidence that the mental model, not the syntax, is doing

48
00:10:46,250 --> 00:10:59,950
the work.
